  "commit": "02813272271f704ffada85fb09216de2afc35b6c",
  "description": "The last of the latent cross-driver defects the matrix uncovered in run/0001 is closed. PostgreSQL now indexes md5(value_string) rather than the column whole, which decision/0011 records along with what it can and cannot serve and the obligation it places on search-filters. decision/0005 had deferred that choice for want of a test to judge by, and was vindicated twice: the test settled it, and reproducing the failure produced PostgreSQL's own hint recommending exactly this remedy. Then fk-index-coverage emptied SCHEMA_AUDIT_FK_INDEX_EXCLUSIONS, so the audit landed two runs ago now asserts the foreign key rule with no exceptions at all -- found six, recorded six, fixed six, excluded none. It passed fourteen checks on a first attempt with no rounds, the guard that made it safe being a check for any index leading with the column rather than one of a chosen name, since InnoDB had already created its own where MySQL was concerned. Also fixed, on main and not in any item, a race this loop introduced in run/0008: a test asserted every cascaded row shared the root's deleted_at, which had been true only while the pinning that guaranteed it existed. Removing that pinning for trashed_batch made the assertion a coin toss on timing, green by luck three times, and PostgreSQL's microsecond precision finally called it. The same flaw sat inside the test written to demonstrate that very failure mode.",
